    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** stands as our premier offensive anchor. With a score of 74.75, his combination of a prime leadoff spot, elite xwOBA (.430), and a significant 14% park factor boost against Freeland makes him a foundational start. Closely trailing him, **Tyler Soderstrom** (70.82) benefits from the most favorable matchup of the day, squaring off against Bratt and his ballooning 7.04 xERA. While **Isaac Paredes** (68.21) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (67.32) provide high-floor production due to strong platoon splits and favorable order positioning, the efficiency of **Brice Turang** (66.10) remains a critical variable; his 92% SB-against rate for the opposing pitcher creates a high-leverage environment for our speed-first strategy, allowing **Brice Turang** to potentially exceed his strong base projection.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Josh Naylor**, who missed the cut with a score of 62.78. Despite a decent projection, the combination of his platoon disadvantage against Abbott and the superior efficiency of our starters at the corner infield slots kept him sidelined. Our optimizer prioritized the high-ceiling upside of **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Austin Riley** to maximize R and RBI potential. Meanwhile, **Henry Bolte** remains a calculated risk; while his score of 58.30 sits near the margin, his exposure to Bratt\u2019s poor xERA and a positive platoon split earned him the final OF spot, even with a sub-optimal #9 lineup slot. We continue to manage our remaining assets like **Corbin Carroll**, **Mickey Moniak**, and **Giancarlo Stanton** carefully, ensuring we preserve our game caps for matchups where the underlying metrics project well above the 40-point floor. No immediate weather hazards are currently flagged, though the relative difficulty of facing tough arms like Peralta and Sears necessitates that our mid-tier starters like **Cole Young** and **Zach McKinstry** maintain plate discipline to avoid losing ground in the AVG category.",
